agents/counselors/1788296923-review-request-question-is-docsphase-1/summary.md:22:  - Significant: Phase 2 will undo it, or Phase 1 behaves wrong
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:7:## 1. Critical: the Mon/Tue escalation rule fires every week, including after a successful Sunday re-auth
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:9:Walk the numbers. Re-auth Sunday 18:30 → `refresh_expires_at` = next Sunday 18:30. Monday 08:00 health run: coming Sunday 18:00 + 12h = Monday 06:00. Sunday 18:30 < Monday 06:00 → nag. Same on Tuesday. The rule as written is a false positive for every properly re-authed token, because a 7-day token issued on Sunday evening always expires within about an hour of the next Sunday email, and any buffer larger than that gap trips it.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:11:The PRD's actual condition is "the re-auth hasn't happened" after the Sunday email. Encode that literally:
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:17:No buffer, no comparison against the coming Sunday. Add a spec that travels to Monday 08:00 after a Sunday 18:30 re-auth and asserts no nag.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:31:- `refresh!` on a non-current row does nothing (reload inside the lock, compare id to `SchwabToken.current.id`, return). Better: have the client resolve the token per request through a class-level accessor so there is no stale object at all.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:66:With re-auth on Sunday evening the token enters the 24h window Saturday evening. The user then gets Saturday 20:00, Sunday 08:00, and the Sunday 18:00 ritual email every single week. Three emails a week for a healthy system trains the user to ignore the one that matters. Send the 24h warning once per token row (dedupe on token id in `token_nag_sent` data), and escalate to twice-daily only when expired, revoked, or absent, or after the Sunday email went unanswered.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:70:- Solid Queue 1.7 interprets schedules without a zone in `config.time_zone`, which is Eastern here, so 08:00, 20:00, and Sunday 18:00 are correct across DST. Put `America/New_York` in the cron strings anyway so a future change to `config.solid_queue.time_zone` cannot silently shift them.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:79:The initializer reads `SCHWAB_ADAPTER` in every environment. One stray dokku config var puts fake positions on a real dashboard and exposes `fake_authorize`. Ignore the env var in production, or raise at boot if the adapter is not `:http`. Guard the fake controller action itself too, not just the route definition.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:81:For Playwright, let `fake_authorize?fail=1` redirect with a bad code. It is simpler than reaching the singleton's queue from another process through the scenarios route.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:92:Against §5.1 and §5.10 the design covers refresh cadence, 7-day expiry from issuance, the 24h notice, pause, catch-up, and the Sunday ritual. Missing or implicit: `sync_resumed` audit on the callback when a pause was active, a nag for the no-token state before first auth, and the token-age metric (`issued_at` exposed for Flow 8). The Resend dependency is the remaining single point of failure: every alert is email. Put the token status line at the top of the morning brief so a dead nag path is still visible daily, and let a failed nag delivery raise so it shows in Solid Queue failed executions on the system page.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:12:`Schwab.client` with no token argument resolves `SchwabToken.current` and raises `NoUsableToken` unless it is usable (`app/models/schwab.rb:12`). Both OAuth entry points call it that way: `authorize` at `app/controllers/schwab_controller.rb:10` and the code exchange at `app/models/schwab_token/reauth.rb:13`. Consequences with the http adapter and a dead token: the authorize link 500s because nothing rescues there, and the callback rescues it as a `Schwab::Error` and renders the failure page reading "no usable Schwab token; re-authenticate" with a Try again link that loops forever. The Sunday ritual works only while the old token is still alive, which is the one case where it matters least. The fake adapter returns the singleton regardless, so every request spec passes, and the spec at `spec/models/schwab_token_spec.rb:123` enshrines the behaviour. OAuth grants carry Basic auth, not a bearer, so they need no token at all. Add `Schwab.oauth_client` returning `Client.new(token: nil, wait: false)` and use it for `authorize_url` and `exchange_code`. Keep `NoUsableToken` for data calls. Add a request spec that forces the http adapter, has no token row, stubs the token endpoint with WebMock, and drives the full callback.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:18:Re-derived from `app/jobs/schwab/token_health_job.rb:25`: nag on Monday or Tuesday when `issued_at` is before the most recent Sunday 18:00 ET. Sunday 18:30 then Monday 08:00: no nag. Wednesday then next Monday and Tuesday: nag both days, at both runs. Because the lifetime is a fixed 168 hours, this predicate is equivalent to "the token dies before the next Sunday email," which is the condition that actually matters. The Sunday 17:00 edge therefore nags on Monday and Tuesday, which is defensible, but the email says "The Sunday re-auth did not happen" while also saying the token was issued 15 hours ago. Reword it to state the consequence: the token expires before next Sunday's email. DST checks out: `time_zone.local` builds 18:00 correctly on both transition Sundays, and `168.hours` is elapsed time. One consequence worth a comment in the code: the fall-back week has 169 hours, so a Sunday 18:30 re-auth on October 25, 2026 expires at 17:30 on November 1, thirty minutes before the email. Nothing false fires, but sync pauses for an hour on a Sunday. No spec covers the 17:00 edge, the DST week, or the 20:00 runs.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:21:The 24-hour warning is once per token now, but a token issued every Sunday at 18:30 enters the window on Saturday evening and triggers the 20:00 run every single week. That is 52 emails a year for a healthy system, on top of the Sunday ritual email. Suppress `:expiring` when `refresh_expires_at` is at or after the next Sunday 18:00, since the ritual email already covers that case. The Sunday 17:00 token still warns, consistently with finding 3.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:27:The dangerous sequence is: row A passes the `current?` check inside the lock, the callback inserts row B during A's HTTP call, and A's refresh comes back `ReauthRequired`. `record_failure` reloads and rechecks `current?` before revoking or pausing (`app/models/schwab_token.rb:70`), so A writes only its error string. Good. The spec at `spec/models/schwab_token_spec.rb:103` tests the early `:stale` return, not this path. It is easy to cover: stub `current?` to return true then false and assert no revocation, no pause, and the error recorded.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:30:Between A's `current?` check in `record_failure` and its write of `sync_paused_reason`, B's transaction can commit and clear the pause, leaving a stale label next to a usable token. Nothing gates on the label, so the cost is a wrong banner. Locking the `SystemSettings` row before the `current?` check serializes it against `issue!` and closes the window.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:42:The `data->>'reason'` clause in `already_warned?` is dead, since the audit writes `reason` as a column. `CatchUpJob` audits `sync_resumed` on every re-auth even when nothing was paused. The index in the migration is on a table that grows by about one row per week. The `- 0` in `most_recent_sunday_email` is noise.
agents/counselors/1788301084-review-request-question-review-build-st/claude-fable.md:51:- Sunday 17:00 re-auth, the November 1, 2026 fall-back week, and the Monday 20:00 run (finding 3).
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:17:- **Filtering on stop price in the job hides trailing stops.** `Position#protected?` already returns false when the price is nil. If the job also filters, a trailing stop and no stop look identical on the dashboard. Assign the newest live stop regardless of price and let the model decide. The UI can then say "trailing stop, price unknown".
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:29:**6. Money-market sweeps become fills, and journal transfers vanish.**
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:30:The fixture already shows a TRADE with a CASH_EQUIVALENT instrument. Mapping TRADE to `fill` will open a trade in SWVXX in step 6. Map to `fill` only when the security is EQUITY, and add `sweep` to the kind enum. JOURNAL is not junk either. A contribution moved from the taxable account into the IRA arrives as JOURNAL, and it is a withdrawal for the flow-adjusted equity series in §5.2. Map JOURNAL with a currency item to deposit or withdrawal by sign, and add `transfer` for JOURNAL or RECEIVE_AND_DELIVER with a security item, since ACAT deliveries are not corporate actions. An activity whose transfer items are all fee items is `fee`. Activities with `status` other than VALID should be stored but never journaled. There is no `status` column, so either add one or keep it in raw and filter there.
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:50:If a job is queued but has not started, the next tick sees a stale started time and enqueues again. Use Solid Queue `limits_concurrency` keyed on account and feed with `to: 1` and discard on conflict. That also covers the slow-prior-run case. Keep the 1-minute slack for cron alignment.
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/claude-fable.md:65:PositionsJob already calls the account endpoint every 15 minutes. AccountsJob only needs `account_numbers` to discover new hashes. On the first sync both accounts land with `trading_enabled` false and nothing journals. The health page in step 7 should show accounts that need operator setup.
agents/counselors/1788300021-review-request-question-before-build-st/summary.md:22:  - 1. Critical: the Mon/Tue escalation rule fires every week, including after a successful Sunday re-auth
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/prompt.md:17:- Models from step 2: Account (schwab_hash, trading_enabled, account_type nullable, raw), Instrument (cusip nullable unique, schwab_instrument_id nullable unique, symbol, symbol_history, predecessor), Position (unique per account+instrument, working_stop_order_id, synced_at, raw; rows absent from the broker are to be deleted), Balance (time series), Order (broker_order_id unique per account, parent/replaces chains, order_proposal_id, placed_via), Activity (broker_activity_id unique per account, kind enum with `other` fallback, order_id, commission, fees, journaled_at), MarketDay (date unique, trading bool, session/regular open and close, source schwab|fallback), SyncRun (feed, status incl. partial and skipped_token, records_touched, unmapped_values jsonb, window_from/to), SystemSettings (sync_paused_reason).
agents/counselors/1788301121-review-request-question-before-build-st/prompt.md:38:- Check the activity kind mapping for cash-account realities (money-market sweeps as TRADE, interest, journal transfers) and what "other" swallows
